    {
      "evidence_id": "pdfplumber:abstract",
      "source": "pdf_layout",
      "field": "abstract",
      "raw_text": "In the last decade, zero-knowledge proof of knowledge pro- tocols have been extensively studied to achieve active security of vari- ous cryptographic protocols. However, the existing solutions simply seek zero-knowledge for both message and randomness, which is an overkill in many applications since protocols may remain secure even if some infor- mation about randomness is leaked to the adversary. We develop this idea to improve the state-of-the-art proof of knowl- edge protocols for RLWE-based public-key encryption and BDLOP com- mitment schemes. In a nutshell, we present new proof of knowledge proto- cols without using noise flooding or rejection sampling which are provably secure under a computational hardness assumption, called Hint-MLWE. We also show an efficient reduction from Hint-MLWE to the standard MLWE assumption. Our approach enjoys the best of two worlds because it has no compu- tational overhead from repetition (abort) and achieves a polynomial over- head between the honest and proven languages. We prove this claim by demonstrating concrete parameters and compare with previous results. Finally, we explain how our idea can be further applied to other proof of knowledge providing advanced functionality.",
      "extractor": "pdfplumber_section_boundary"
    },
